WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.869 --> 00:00:02.910
LIZ FONG-JONES: Hi there.

00:00:02.910 --> 00:00:06.280
I'm Liz, a Site Reliability
Engineer, or SRE, at Google.

00:00:06.280 --> 00:00:07.860
And I teach Google
Cloud customers

00:00:07.860 --> 00:00:10.159
how to build and operate
reliable services.

00:00:10.159 --> 00:00:11.700
SETH VARGO: And I'm
Seth, a Developer

00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:14.880
Advocate at Google focused on
infrastructure and operations.

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:17.890
And Liz and I are here to
settle things once and for all.

00:00:17.890 --> 00:00:20.375
Which is better, DevOps or SRE?

00:00:20.375 --> 00:00:21.750
LIZ FONG-JONES:
Whoa there, Seth.

00:00:21.750 --> 00:00:22.494
Hold on a second.

00:00:22.494 --> 00:00:23.910
I'm not sure you're
really looking

00:00:23.910 --> 00:00:25.440
at this in the right way.

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:27.900
But first of all, maybe we
should clarify some things.

00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:29.577
What do you think DevOps is?

00:00:29.577 --> 00:00:31.410
SETH VARGO: So that's
a great question, Liz.

00:00:31.410 --> 00:00:34.170
Back in the day,
operators and developers

00:00:34.170 --> 00:00:35.580
had a lot of contention.

00:00:35.580 --> 00:00:37.830
Developers used to
throw their code

00:00:37.830 --> 00:00:40.234
over the metaphorical
wall, and operators

00:00:40.234 --> 00:00:42.150
were responsible for
keeping that code running

00:00:42.150 --> 00:00:43.320
in production.

00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:45.420
Operators had little
understanding of the code

00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:47.610
bases, and developers
had little understanding

00:00:47.610 --> 00:00:49.289
of operational practices.

00:00:49.289 --> 00:00:51.330
But developers were
concerned with shipping code,

00:00:51.330 --> 00:00:54.010
and operators were
concerned with reliability.

00:00:54.010 --> 00:00:55.800
This misalignment
often caused tension

00:00:55.800 --> 00:00:57.739
within the organization.

00:00:57.739 --> 00:00:59.780
LIZ FONG-JONES: So if I
understand you correctly,

00:00:59.780 --> 00:01:00.970
you're saying that
the developers were

00:01:00.970 --> 00:01:03.070
responsible for features,
and the operators

00:01:03.070 --> 00:01:06.100
were responsible for stability,
meaning the developers wanted

00:01:06.100 --> 00:01:08.410
to move faster to get
their features out faster

00:01:08.410 --> 00:01:11.787
and the operators wanted to move
slower to keep things stable?

00:01:11.787 --> 00:01:13.870
I could see how that would
cause a lot of tension.

00:01:13.870 --> 00:01:14.830
SETH VARGO: Exactly.

00:01:14.830 --> 00:01:18.370
So DevOps is a set of practices
and a culture designed

00:01:18.370 --> 00:01:21.850
to break down those barriers
between developers, operators,

00:01:21.850 --> 00:01:24.130
and other parts of
the organization.

00:01:24.130 --> 00:01:27.260
I break DevOps down
into five key areas.

00:01:27.260 --> 00:01:30.220
First, reduce
organizational silence.

00:01:30.220 --> 00:01:32.290
By breaking down
barriers across teams,

00:01:32.290 --> 00:01:35.280
we can increase collaboration
and thorough put.

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:38.380
Second, accept
failure as normal.

00:01:38.380 --> 00:01:40.910
Computers are
inherently unreliable,

00:01:40.910 --> 00:01:42.730
so we can't expect perfection.

00:01:42.730 --> 00:01:44.920
And when we introduce
humans into the system,

00:01:44.920 --> 00:01:47.480
we get even more imperfection.

00:01:47.480 --> 00:01:50.300
Third, implement gradual change.

00:01:50.300 --> 00:01:54.350
Not only are small, incremental
changes easier to review,

00:01:54.350 --> 00:01:57.230
but in the event that
a gradual change does

00:01:57.230 --> 00:01:59.210
make a bug in
production, it allows

00:01:59.210 --> 00:02:01.130
us to reduce our
mean time to recover,

00:02:01.130 --> 00:02:03.500
making it simple to roll back.

00:02:03.500 --> 00:02:07.340
Fourth, we need to leverage
tooling and automation.

00:02:07.340 --> 00:02:10.430
And fifth, we need to
measure everything.

00:02:10.430 --> 00:02:12.920
Measurement is a critical
gauge for success.

00:02:12.920 --> 00:02:15.470
And without a way to measure
if our first four pillars

00:02:15.470 --> 00:02:19.800
were successful, we would have
no way of knowing if they were.

00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:23.990
So, Liz, you've been an SRE at
Google for over 10 years now.

00:02:23.990 --> 00:02:26.570
Do you think any of the way
that I described DevOps aligns

00:02:26.570 --> 00:02:28.790
with your experience as an SRE?

00:02:28.790 --> 00:02:30.890
LIZ FONG-JONES: It's
sounding very familiar.

00:02:30.890 --> 00:02:34.120
Because, if you think about
DevOps as a philosophy,

00:02:34.120 --> 00:02:38.270
SRE is a prescriptive way of
accomplishing that philosophy.

00:02:38.270 --> 00:02:41.960
So if DevOps were an interface
in programming language,

00:02:41.960 --> 00:02:45.020
you might almost say that
SRE is a concrete class that

00:02:45.020 --> 00:02:46.910
implements DevOps.

00:02:46.910 --> 00:02:48.930
Let's take a look
at how that is.

00:02:48.930 --> 00:02:51.230
So, Seth, when you
talked about eliminating

00:02:51.230 --> 00:02:53.390
organizational silos,
what I thought about

00:02:53.390 --> 00:02:55.610
is the fact that we share
ownership of production

00:02:55.610 --> 00:02:56.900
with our developers.

00:02:56.900 --> 00:02:58.400
And we use the same
tooling in order

00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:01.010
to make sure everyone has the
same view and same approach

00:03:01.010 --> 00:03:02.522
to working with production.

00:03:02.522 --> 00:03:04.730
When you talked about
accepting accidents and failure

00:03:04.730 --> 00:03:07.160
as normal, what I thought
about is the fact that--

00:03:07.160 --> 00:03:09.740
similar to many
DevOps practitioners--

00:03:09.740 --> 00:03:11.300
we have blameless
postmortems, where

00:03:11.300 --> 00:03:14.240
we make sure that the failures
that happen in our production

00:03:14.240 --> 00:03:17.600
systems don't happen the
exact same way more than once.

00:03:17.600 --> 00:03:20.120
And we accept the failures
as normal by encoding

00:03:20.120 --> 00:03:23.000
a concept of an error budget of
how much the system is allowed

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:24.290
to go out of spec.

00:03:24.290 --> 00:03:26.941
And then third, we talked
about making gradual changes.

00:03:26.941 --> 00:03:28.940
And when you said that,
I thought about the fact

00:03:28.940 --> 00:03:30.515
that we canary
things, that we roll

00:03:30.515 --> 00:03:32.390
things out to a small
percentage of the fleet

00:03:32.390 --> 00:03:34.159
before we move them
out for all users.

00:03:34.159 --> 00:03:36.200
And then fourth, when you
talked about leveraging

00:03:36.200 --> 00:03:38.210
tooling and automation,
what I thought about

00:03:38.210 --> 00:03:40.250
is the fact that we try
to eliminate manual work

00:03:40.250 --> 00:03:41.310
as much as possible.

00:03:41.310 --> 00:03:43.970
So we measure how
much toil we have,

00:03:43.970 --> 00:03:46.522
and then we try to automate
this year's job away.

00:03:46.522 --> 00:03:48.980
And then fifth, when you talked
about measuring everything,

00:03:48.980 --> 00:03:50.780
I thought about exactly
that measurement

00:03:50.780 --> 00:03:52.820
of measuring the amount
of toil that we have

00:03:52.820 --> 00:03:55.849
and measuring the reliability
and health of our systems.

00:03:55.849 --> 00:03:57.140
SETH VARGO: I really like that.

00:03:57.140 --> 00:03:59.390
Class SRE implements DevOps.

00:03:59.390 --> 00:04:01.340
We should get that on
a shirt or something.

00:04:01.340 --> 00:04:04.070
But just like a class in
a programming language,

00:04:04.070 --> 00:04:06.440
there might be additional
functions or methods

00:04:06.440 --> 00:04:08.930
that don't necessarily
correspond to that interface.

00:04:08.930 --> 00:04:11.390
Or the class might implement
multiple interfaces.

00:04:11.390 --> 00:04:12.924
Do you think SRE is like that?

00:04:12.924 --> 00:04:14.340
LIZ FONG-JONES: I
absolutely think

00:04:14.340 --> 00:04:16.190
that's the case
because of the fact

00:04:16.190 --> 00:04:19.370
that SRE doesn't do things
in the exact same way

00:04:19.370 --> 00:04:21.920
that other people that
implement DevOps elsewhere

00:04:21.920 --> 00:04:23.670
might want to do.

00:04:23.670 --> 00:04:26.330
So we'll talk a little bit
more about those differences,

00:04:26.330 --> 00:04:29.060
such as how exactly
SLOs work, which

00:04:29.060 --> 00:04:32.270
are a very specific concept
that we implement in order

00:04:32.270 --> 00:04:34.640
to make SRE successful.

00:04:34.640 --> 00:04:35.390
SETH VARGO: Great.

00:04:35.390 --> 00:04:36.920
Well, that settles it, then.

00:04:36.920 --> 00:04:40.730
It turns out that DevOps and SRE
aren't two competing methods,

00:04:40.730 --> 00:04:42.800
but rather close
friends designed

00:04:42.800 --> 00:04:45.170
to help break down
organizational barriers

00:04:45.170 --> 00:04:47.720
to deliver better
software faster.

00:04:47.720 --> 00:04:49.486
Thank you, everyone,
for watching.

00:04:49.486 --> 00:04:51.110
Please be sure to
check the description

00:04:51.110 --> 00:04:52.910
below for more links,
and don't forget

00:04:52.910 --> 00:04:54.680
to subscribe to our channel.

00:04:54.680 --> 00:04:56.300
Stay tuned for our
next video, where

00:04:56.300 --> 00:05:01.900
we will discuss the differences
between SLIs, SLOs, and SLAs.

